#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e831a23-ec58-57c0-b08a-10f1936c0841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354234} {:answer/id #uuid "eed4fa3d-ca16-5a44-a65e-da0e02f4853c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354235} {:answer/id #uuid "a37aeded-9bcc-5dea-b427-9c06442ba73a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354236} {:answer/id #uuid "4983a346-cbaf-59f1-833e-14e3d492549a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354237} {:answer/id #uuid "5eebd6b6-65b8-5ecb-b8b5-3fed8d561976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eebd6b6-65b8-5ecb-b8b5-3fed8d561976" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354238} :answer-field/id #uuid "b190eda4-0caa-5dc2-a98a-1322d9b66a73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa4a3d4f-86d7-526d-8b7d-9356bb095979" :question/math-academy-id "q-118776" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=3+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2(3+0)}-\\frac{1}{2(3+{a}^{2})}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd0b8ec2-e548-5200-a362-c9139d70275a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354241} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a46bbe-4777-5066-b5a3-5103698bef72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354242} {:answer/id #uuid "f28539e6-2dbd-58bb-9599-64198317af32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354243} {:answer/id #uuid "0b88e548-8a1e-5925-8aac-99ade3b148d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354244} {:answer/id #uuid "cd82ee17-fa8c-549c-a03a-f9a0799f9870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd82ee17-fa8c-549c-a03a-f9a0799f9870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354245} :answer-field/id #uuid "93e72a3e-09bd-544e-aa7f-fa5fd9aa8792" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1104938-8add-5c20-aaed-a503fbe2493d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118777" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{3})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot [0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffe2c351-4438-5626-84b7-523a1e0a0461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354248} {:answer/id #uuid "bce81980-d4c6-51f4-a08b-bace845047cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354249} {:answer/id #uuid "84b84f50-e25f-59d1-89dd-1f9a117bdbb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354250} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8c381f-586b-5a32-a993-8f860bd663d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354251} {:answer/id #uuid "800d37d1-b4ef-5db7-9566-876a4ab33a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84b84f50-e25f-59d1-89dd-1f9a117bdbb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354250} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0ef435-1c50-5611-bca4-5634a0380660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2dfa35ce-beed-58f8-8439-6e601518f0e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-20797" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{3}$,  then $\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}+C \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{({e}^{{x}^{3}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{{a}^{3}}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c4fa1cb-a166-5ac0-8db4-342c00bdd9fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354255} {:answer/id #uuid "43e22a05-9c84-50a4-a49b-e3b90ffd284e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354256} {:answer/id #uuid "15bc5a5d-5f32-5827-95aa-cea376046798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354257} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebb3d87-0b26-538f-9887-ee0670dee379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354258} {:answer/id #uuid "1045e7e0-b7da-55ad-be68-d1290286416b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1045e7e0-b7da-55ad-be68-d1290286416b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354259} :answer-field/id #uuid "d624deec-d629-5e5a-8626-3caa4566f007" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68984b43-c28b-51c9-a2e8-e7cd0fb9d27d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20804" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{9+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{u}{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{0}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(0-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =0-\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e57e08e3-0943-5768-b777-27020d94b29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103728} {:answer/id #uuid "f848ea7d-d808-53ed-8acd-7ca74d6a960f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103729} {:answer/id #uuid "1028ba03-1712-550c-8905-39f82292d7ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103730} {:answer/id #uuid "279f6232-3dce-5484-86bd-403603bc5d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103731} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2f9e91-cfb5-5587-a048-8b00437b617e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "279f6232-3dce-5484-86bd-403603bc5d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103731} :answer-field/id #uuid "043267e0-6d4c-57fd-8d01-6000fca71dc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9384e7ca-141a-535c-ac99-8b9073bc22b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-30967" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=-{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=-2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{-{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
